## Verify the venv

```bash
# inside the venv
python3 -c "import cudf, cuml, cupy; print('cuml', cuml.__version__, '| gpu', cupy.cuda.runtime.getDeviceCount())"
# expect: cuml 26.02.000 | gpu 1
```

## If RAPIDS was already pip-installed into system Python (cleanup)

The first attempt here fell back to a `--user` install because `python3-venv` was missing, leaving
RAPIDS and CUDA-12 bindings in `~/.local`. After the venv works, clean the system env so Step 4 is
unambiguous. Run with the **venv deactivated**:

```bash
deactivate 2>/dev/null
pip uninstall -y cudf-cu12 cuml-cu12 cupy-cuda12x \
    libcudf-cu12 libcuml-cu12 pylibcudf-cu12 librmm-cu12 rmm-cu12 \
    libraft-cu12 pylibraft-cu12 libkvikio-cu12

# confirm Step 4's runtime is intact
python3 -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available())"
# expect: 2.14.1+cu130 True
```

If that torch check ever prints False or errors, restore the bindings PyTorch wants:

```bash
pip install --user --force-reinstall 'cuda-bindings>=13.0.3' 'cuda-toolkit==13.0.3'
```

## Running Step 3

CPU (no venv needed; system Python):
```bash
python3 3_node_embeddings.py --method xgboost --graph-dir haplograph/genome --device cpu \
    --xgb-trees 100 --xgb-depth 4
```

XGBoost native GPU, SVD on CPU (no venv needed):
```bash
python3 3_node_embeddings.py --method xgboost --graph-dir haplograph/genome --device cuda \
    --xgb-trees 100 --xgb-depth 4
```

RAPIDS GPU SVD (**inside the venv**):
```bash
source ~/rapids-env/bin/activate
python3 3_node_embeddings.py --method xgboost --graph-dir haplograph/genome --device cuda --rapids \
    --xgb-trees 100 --xgb-depth 4 --xgb-svd-nodes 150000 --chunk-nodes 100000
deactivate
```

Notes:
- `--rapids` only accelerates the two SVD steps (carrier-matrix SVD and leaf-basis SVD).
  Tree-fit uses XGBoost's own GPU path (`--device cuda`); everything else is CPU numpy.
- The carrier SVD is fit on a `--rapids-fit-rows` sample (default 150k) then projected in
  `--rapids-chunk` dense chunks (default 200k). The sampling is an approximation: on the genome
  run it gave val-accuracy 0.986 vs 0.992/0.993 for the exact CPU SVD. Raise `--rapids-fit-rows`
  (e.g. 300000) to close that gap — the carrier matrix has only ~2,548 columns, so a larger fit
  sample is cheap.
- Delete any cached `features_svd*.npy` in the graph-dir before benchmarking `--rapids`, or the
  cached CPU features load and the GPU SVD never runs.

## Why RAPIDS here, honestly

This step is CPU/RAM-bound, not GPU-bound — `--rapids` is a demonstration of the NVIDIA
data-science stack (cuML on the RTX PRO 6000), not a large speedup. The dominant cost is the
CPU-side chunked embedding of all 11M nodes, which `--rapids` does not change. Report the measured
times and the accuracy together; the SVD moving to GPU is the genuine RAPIDS contribution.
